// Melodrama: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== melodrama.f01 | feature | two-part
title: The Token
genre: melodrama + period-drama
tone: Fog-soft, aching and unashamed of tears
era: 1758 | period
setting: A foundling hospital on the edge of London and the streets that feed it | real | institution
logline: A servant girl who gives up her newborn at a hospital for abandoned children leaves half of an embroidered ribbon as the token by which she may one day reclaim him, and spends eleven years earning the right to come back with the other half.
premise: The hospital takes infants no questions asked, and gives each a new name. A mother may leave a token: a coin, a scrap of cloth, a button, pinned to the admission paper and sealed. If she ever returns, describes it and can pay for the child's keep, he is hers again. Almost none do. {sarah} cuts a ribbon she embroidered with a bird, and keeps the half with the wing.
synopsis: Part one is the leaving. {sarah} is nineteen, dismissed without a character by the family whose son is the father. She walks to the hospital on the day of a public reception, when women draw coloured balls from a bag to learn whether their child will be taken: white for admitted, black for refused. The film stays a long time with the bag. She draws white, and is not sure it is the luck. A clerk, {mr_collin}, kind and procedural, cuts and pins the ribbon. The boy becomes a number and a new name, and is sent to a wet nurse in the country. Part two runs on two tracks eleven years long. {sarah} works: a laundry, a cookshop, at last her own stall of pies by the river, each coin counted against a sum the hospital has never told her. She does not marry, because she would have to explain. The boy, {tom}, grows in the hospital's order and music: brown uniform, a choir trained by a great composer's bequest, a future as a ship's boy or apprentice. He is happy enough; he has a friend, {ned_f}; he believes, as all the children privately do, that his mother was a lady. A governor's wife, {lady_h}, takes a fancy to his voice and speaks of placing him in her household. The crisis is of the kind this genre exists for. {sarah} comes on a visiting Sunday, when the public pays to hear the children sing, simply to look. She knows him at once by his ear. He sings a solo, and looks straight through her. To claim him she must pay eleven years' keep, and take him from a prospect with a great family to a pie stall. {mr_collin}, grey now, tells her the sum, and then, improperly, how few have ever paid it. She goes away. She comes back in the last reel with a purse and a strip of ribbon. The packet is unsealed. Two halves of a bird. The film lets the boy be bewildered and angry before it lets him be anything else, and it ends not on an embrace but on a walk: a woman and a boy in a brown uniform crossing the fields toward the smoke of the city, three feet apart, then two.
question: Is love the right to a child, or what you do without him?
themes: poverty as the only sin; the thread kept; institutions that do good and cannot do this
audience: Adults; anyone prepared to cry
viewpoint: Hers and the boy's, braided
stakes: A child's future, a mother's whole life's work, and whether they can be anything to each other
conflict: A mother's eleven-year labour against the price of her own child and the better prospect he would give up
ending: She pays, the ribbon matches, and he goes with her, unwillingly at first. They are walking home.
experience: A lump in the throat that does not go, music used shamelessly and well, and an ending that earns its hope
look: Brown wool uniforms in white corridors; fog and river mud; a ribbon's colours the brightest thing in the film
sound: Boys' voices in a chapel; a pie-seller's cry; a seal being broken
m: restore | institution | love-vs-duty | reconciled
@ sarah | Sarah Bender | lead | protagonist | human | she/her | 19 | Her son back | She has the half with the wing | Eleven years' keep, and what she would be taking him from | Dogged, proud, tender, close-mouthed | From a girl with nothing to a woman with a purse and a doubt | Plain, country vowels, few words | Mob cap, burnt forearms, a ribbon sewn into her stays
@ tom | Tom Field | co-lead | deuteragonist | human | he/him | 11 | A place in the world, and the solo | He has been told he is nobody's | A stranger says she is his mother | Bright, guarded, loyal to his friend | From a hospital's boy to a pie-seller's son, slowly | Careful, taught speech; sings | Brown uniform, red trim, one ear that sticks out
@ mr_collin | Mr. Collin | supporting | mentor | human | he/him | 40 | The rules kept, kindly | He has pinned four thousand tokens | He is not supposed to hope | Meticulous, gentle, tired | From clerk to the one who breaks a small rule | Procedural, soft | Ink-stained cuffs, a billet book, sealing wax
@ lady_h | Lady Harcourt-Vane | supporting | antagonist | human | she/her | 45 | The boy with the voice, for her household | She means it as a kindness | He has a mother | Gracious, acquisitive, not wicked | From patroness to a carriage leaving | Musical, assured | Grey silk, a quizzing glass
@ ned_f | Ned | featured | ally | human | he/him | 11 | Not to be left behind | Tom is all the family he has | Nobody is coming for him | Cheeky, brave, hiding it | From friend to the one who says go | Whispered jokes | Brown uniform, a tooth missing
~ sarah - tom | mother and son | a ribbon cut in two | three feet apart, then two
~ sarah - mr_collin | petitioner and clerk | a token pinned and sealed | a sum named, and an improper kindness
~ tom - ned_f | two foundlings | a pact that their mothers were ladies | "go on, then"
~ tom - lady_h | chorister and patroness | a prospect | a prospect given up
